  "description": "A quick, practical guide to Language Processing: Decoding Speech.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "You hear a stranger speak and decode meaning from vibrating air in real time. No computer can match this feat.",
      "visual": "🗣️"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "The auditory cortex breaks speech into phonemes. Then Wernicke's area in the temporal lobe extracts meaning.",
      "visual": "📖"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Broca's area in the frontal lobe handles speech production. Damage here causes halting, effortful speech.",
      "visual": "🔇"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Babies can distinguish all human speech sounds. By age one, they lose sensitivity to sounds not in their native language.",
      "visual": "👶"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Sign languages activate the same brain regions as spoken languages, proving language is modality-independent.",
      "visual": "🤟"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Language is not about ears or mouths.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What happens to babies' speech sound discrimination by age one?",
    "options": [
      "They lose sensitivity to non-native sounds",
      "They gain sensitivity to all languages",
      "Nothing changes"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
